-- Version 31 → 32: epics, as a generic parent link (LNL-55).
--
-- Two new columns on `issues` and nothing else. `parent_id` is a nullable
-- self-reference — the epic an issue belongs under, or NULL for none — and
-- `child_order` is the issue's manual rank among its siblings under that parent.
-- An issue that has children is an epic; there is no separate "epic" kind. See
-- Issues.sq's parent_id / child_order for why parent_id is single-column and how
-- the same-project / one-level / no-cycle rules live in IssueRepository rather
-- than the schema.
--
-- ── Nothing to back-fill ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- No issue gets a parent: NULL is "belongs under no epic", an absence rather than
-- a row, so every existing issue is correctly parentless and every existing board
-- is untouched — indistinguishable from the state before these columns existed.
-- child_order is NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, which back-fills every existing row as
-- unranked (0), exactly as sort_order does; 9.sqm's and 30.sqm's reasoning for a
-- DEFAULT that reproduces the prior state applies verbatim.
--
-- ── Plain ALTER TABLE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- parent_id is a single-column nullable reference, the exact case SQLite's ALTER
-- TABLE ADD COLUMN accepts — 29.sqm's planned_version_id is the same shape. The
-- default is omitted because SQLite REQUIRES the default of an added column with a
-- REFERENCES clause to be NULL. child_order is NOT NULL with a constant default,
-- which ADD COLUMN also accepts. The self reference resolves against `issues`,
-- which already exists, so no ordering concern like 29.sqm's.
--
-- The two ALTERs must leave the schema MigrationTest reads back — column type,
-- nullability, default, and the parent_id foreign key's ON DELETE SET NULL —
-- identical to what Issues.sq now declares; it applies this to the 31 snapshot and
-- compares column for column and foreign key for foreign key against the .sq files.

ALTER TABLE issues ADD COLUMN parent_id INTEGER REFERENCES issues(id) ON DELETE SET NULL;

ALTER TABLE issues ADD COLUMN child_order INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
